he latest MT4 test shows that using one weighted average maturity per bucket increases CSRBB HVaR by €0.96m (4.28%) relative to midpoint discounting. Validation extended the test by using separate weighted average maturities for assets and liabilities, which increased HVaR by €1.37m (6.12%). Using individual projected payment dates across the full historical shock set increased HVaR by €1.52m (6.77%), with the existing curve mappings held fixed. The same two historical shocks drove the 99.9th percentile. The increase came from larger losses under those shocks.

On both shock dates, approximately 96% of the difference between the split weighted average and midpoint runs came from the 4–5 and 5–6 year liability buckets, which are dominated by AT1. This points to the discounting of large AT1 principal repayments, whose projected timing depends on assumed call dates, as the main driver of the difference.

Validation challenges the choice of midpoint discounting and the TDD’s rationale for treating it as conservative. Midpoints produced a higher estimate than the combined weighted average in an earlier delivery and a lower estimate in the latest delivery. The direction of the difference therefore depends on the portfolio, and the available evidence does not support a general claim of conservatism.
